WEBVTT

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.920
Welcome to Education Session 2: orders and market types.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:12.330
Today: stop loss, take profit, and pending orders. The tools that let you plan and control every trade.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.540
First, the simplest order: a market order.

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.850
It opens a trade immediately, at the best available price.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:29.200
When you buy, you pay the ask price. When you sell, you receive the bid price. The gap between them is the spread.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:38.720
A stop loss is an order that closes your trade automatically, if the price moves against you to a level you choose.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:45.240
Its purpose is to limit the loss on a trade, decided in advance, not in the heat of the moment.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:55.560
Example: you buy EUR/USD at 1.1000, with a stop loss at 1.0950, fifty pips away.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:00.610
On 1 lot, that's a planned maximum loss of about $500.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:08.810
In fast markets, or when prices gap over a weekend, a stop can be filled at a worse price. This is called slippage.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:12.690
Where should a stop go?

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:22.830
Common practice is to place it where your trade idea is proven wrong. For example, just beyond a recent low, or a support or resistance level.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:29.170
Too close, and normal price noise can stop you out. Too far, and the loss becomes too large.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.390
Many traders choose the stop first, and then size the trade.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.540
For example, risking 1% of a $10,000 account is $100.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:45.390
With a 50-pip stop, at $10 per pip per lot, that's 0.2 lots.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:54.080
A take profit does the opposite. It closes the trade automatically, when the price reaches your target.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:02:00.430
It locks in the gain, even when you aren't watching the screen, so a winning trade isn't given back.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:12.600
Example: you buy at 1.1000, with a take profit at 1.1100. That's 100 pips, about $1,000 on 1 lot.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:20.690
Compared with the 50-pip stop, you risk 50 pips to make 100. That's a risk-to-reward ratio of 1 to 2.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:32.900
Targets should fit your strategy. A short-term trader may aim for nearby levels; a longer-term trader, for bigger moves. What matters is that the target is realistic for that market.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:36.960
Now, pending orders.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:45.650
A market order trades now. A pending order is an instruction to open a trade later, only if the price reaches a level you set.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.800
There are four main types.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:53.640
A Buy Limit buys below the current price, when you expect a dip first, and then a rise.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:59.450
A Sell Limit sells above the current price, when you expect a rise first, and then a fall.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.210
A Buy Stop buys above the current price, when you expect a breakout to keep going up.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:11.070
A Sell Stop sells below the current price, when you expect a breakdown to keep going down.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:17.810
An easy way to remember: limit orders look for a better price. Stop orders follow momentum.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:27.380
You can attach a stop loss and a take profit to a pending order, so the whole plan is set before the trade even opens.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:31.140
Planning entries in advance has real advantages.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:39.460
You don't need to watch the screen. You avoid emotional, last-minute decisions. And every trade starts with a defined risk.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:47.660
Keep in mind: a pending order may never be triggered, and in fast markets it can be filled at a different price than requested.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.260
Let's recap.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.980
A market order trades immediately, at the current price.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:59.590
A stop loss limits your loss. A take profit locks in your gain.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:07.150
Pending orders open trades at the levels you choose: limit orders for a better price, stop orders for momentum.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.510
In Session 3, we'll look at two advanced strategies: hedging and averaging.
